From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 22:57:57 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] x87: an unmasked overflow or underflow rebiases a register
 result

While the control word unmasks overflow or underflow, x87 delivers an
out-of-range result to a register with its significand rounded as if
the exponent were unbounded, at the control word's precision, and its
exponent brought back into range by 24576: down for an overflow, up
for an underflow, which an exact tiny result raises too. A result
still out of range after that overflows as if masked or becomes a
zero. The helpers left the masked result, an infinity or a denormal.

SoftFloat's state gains rebias bits, softfloat_rebias_overflow and
softfloat_rebias_underflow, which only softfloat_roundPackToExtF80
reads, so conversions to other formats, and so stores, never rebias.
The x87 helpers set them from the control word.

FSCALE built 2^n with exp2l and multiplied by it, so a scale beyond the
format's own exponent range gave an infinity or a zero where the
result itself is in range, such as 1.5 * 2^-16382 scaled by 20000. A
new SoftFloat routine, extF80_scale, adds the truncated scale to the
exponent and rounds once at full precision; a scale of zero leaves
even a denormal alone, as on a Xeon 8581C.
